export default "The belief that you should not exercise during your period does not have a physiological basis for most people. It persists partly from cultural norms and partly from confusing \"rest because you are exhausted\" (appropriate, individual) with \"do not exercise because it is your period\" (not supported by research).\n\nWhat the research actually shows is the opposite. Exercise is one of the evidence-backed interventions for period symptoms.\n\n## Why Exercise Helps Cramps\n\nMenstrual cramping is caused by prostaglandins, lipid compounds that cause uterine vasoconstriction and muscle contraction. Several things happen when you exercise during menstruation that directly counteract this.\n\n**Endorphin release:** Moderate aerobic exercise triggers endorphin release within 10-15 minutes. Endorphins act as natural analgesics through opioid receptor binding. They also modulate the cyclooxygenase pathway, reducing prostaglandin signaling. This is why a 20-30 minute walk often reduces cramp severity. It is not distraction. It is pharmacology.\n\n**Improved blood flow:** Prostaglandins cause uterine vasoconstriction. Exercise causes systemic vasodilation. Improved uterine blood flow reduces the ischemic component of cramping.\n\n**Cortisol reduction:** Moderate exercise reduces cortisol over time. Cortisol amplifies prostaglandin production. This is more relevant for chronic exercise patterns than for a single session.\n\n## Light vs. High-Intensity: Does It Matter?\n\nFor most people, exercise intensity during menstruation is a question of energy and comfort, not medical risk.\n\n**Light-to-moderate exercise** (walking, yoga, swimming, easy cycling, gentle strength training): consistently beneficial in the research. Endorphin release is achieved at moderate intensity. You do not need to push hard to get the pain-relief effect.\n\n**High-intensity exercise** (HIIT, interval running, heavy strength training): safe for most people with primary period pain. Some research suggests it may temporarily increase flow volume in some individuals because intense exercise can raise prostaglandin production. This is not harmful, but if you notice heavier flow on days of intense training, reducing intensity on the heaviest days is reasonable.\n\n**For endometriosis or adenomyosis:** High-intensity exercise may worsen pelvic pain in some people with these conditions. The proposed mechanism is increased retrograde menstrual flow with intense intra-abdominal pressure, plus elevated prostaglandins from intense training. This is individual. Some people with endometriosis exercise intensely without worsening symptoms. Others find it significantly painful. Let your actual pain response guide you rather than a rule.\n\n## Iron: The Hidden Variable\n\nHeavy periods (more than 80mL per cycle) deplete iron over time. Iron-deficiency anemia produces fatigue, reduced oxygen-carrying capacity, and reduced exercise tolerance that compounds period fatigue.\n\nIf exercise during your period feels exhausting in a way that seems out of proportion, consider getting serum ferritin tested. Ferritin below 30 ng/mL warrants treatment even without anemia symptoms. Iron-rich foods such as red meat, lentils with vitamin C, and spinach with lemon in the days around and after your period support replenishment.\n\nExercising while iron-deficient does not harm you, but it does feel significantly harder. The fatigue is real.\n\n## Specific Exercise Types During Menstruation\n\n**Yoga:** Particularly useful. Poses that decompress the lower abdomen (child's pose, cat-cow, reclined bound angle, supine twist) provide direct comfort during cramping. Inverted poses such as headstand and shoulder stand are sometimes said to affect flow. There is no good evidence they cause harm. They are personal preference, not a medical concern.\n\n**Swimming:** Consistently cited as beneficial. The horizontal position reduces hydrostatic pressure differences. The cool water and movement provide comfort. Period underwear, menstrual cups, and tampons all work for swimming.\n\n**Running:** Completely safe. Some people find running worsens cramping initially because the jostling can feel uncomfortable during heavy cramping. Others find it provides the most effective cramp relief of any exercise. Both experiences are valid.\n\n**Strength training:** Safe. Performance may be slightly lower on very heavy cramping days. There is no joint or safety concern. The follicular-phase performance advantage starts after bleeding ends. That is a performance consideration, not a safety one.\n\n**HIIT:** Safe for primary period pain. See the intensity note above for endometriosis and adenomyosis.\n\n## The Permission Question\n\nMany people ask whether they are allowed to exercise during their period, or allowed to rest. The better frame is: listen to your body's actual signals.\n\nIf you are genuinely fatigued, rest. If you are not fatigued and want to move, move. It will probably help. The cycle syncing framework suggests rest during menstruation as a phase-based recommendation. The physiology supports it as an option, not a requirement.\n\nThe performance reality is that menstruation (Day 1-5) is not when hormones most favor output. That is the follicular phase. But \"not the best phase for peak performance\" is not the same as \"you should not exercise.\"\n\n## What This Means for Floriva Users\n\nLogging how exercise affects your energy and symptoms during menstruation across 2-3 cycles gives you your own data. Does a 30-minute walk reduce cramping? Does intense training worsen flow? These are individual questions that population research can't answer precisely for you. Consistent tracking reveals the pattern.\n## Medical Context\n\nThis page is educational and is not medical advice. Use it to prepare questions and track patterns; diagnosis, treatment decisions, supplement dosing, and medication use should be reviewed with a qualified clinician, especially if pain is severe, bleeding is heavy, cycles change suddenly, pregnancy is possible, or you have a known condition.";
